Do not send conceptual artwork directly to production. **Production requires:**
confirmed dimensions; correct front and back orientation; safe zones and bleed where required;
suitable colour mode and resolution; outlined or supplied fonts; linked or embedded assets;
barcode space; legible mandatory text; approved version identification; final proof at actual
size.

overall artwork size; front and back panel width;
wrap or overlap allowance; bleed and safe zone; seam or curve position; barcode size and quiet
area; variable-data zone; minimum readable text; cutter or dieline reference where relevant.

**An editable file should include:** organised layers; named groups; current logo asset;
editable text or properly supplied fonts; linked or embedded images; defined dimensions;
colour specification; version identifier; no hidden unused alternatives; an exported proof for
comparison.

preserve bottle shape; preserve label artwork; preserve cap or pump;
preserve product colour; preserve size relationships; preserve approved contents and quantity
presentation.

**Quality:** sufficient resolution for the intended channel; controlled focus and sharpness;
natural or deliberately controlled lighting; clean edges; realistic shadows and reflections; no
accidental background artefacts; correct crop and aspect ratio.

**Consistency — product families should share:** camera or visual angle logic; scale;
background family; shadow treatment; margin system; image sequence; identity placement.

Customer-first does not mean "the customer is always technically correct." It
means: listen before concluding; understand the customer's intended outcome; use clear language;
reduce avoidable effort; protect safety and fairness; explain limits honestly; own the handoff;
use evidence to improve.

**Repeat depends on:** product performance; consistent quality; availability;
value; ease; trust; support; memory of the complete experience. Retention messages cannot
compensate for a weak first experience.

**Product Experience Signals — collect:** wrong-product selection; directions questions;
performance feedback; sensory preference; damage/leakage; delivery issue; return; repeat timing;
product availability; review themes; support contact. **Classify preference, expectation
mismatch, misuse, product failure, and delivery failure separately.**

Customer communication should be: clear; accurate; respectful; relevant;
consistent with current product and policy; appropriately warm; free from unnecessary jargon;
explicit about next steps. Do not sound certain when evidence is uncertain.

**Listen for:** what happened; what the customer expected; product/order/account identity;
timing; impact; what outcome they want; evidence available; safety urgency; prior contacts.
Summarise the issue back before deciding.

